#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475991} :activity/id #uuid "9c7a70fd-abbe-516b-95be-41d0d36b0d99" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3952c809-b36e-5c0a-8130-4cc5c4fb5968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324433} {:answer/id #uuid "43c02d07-8760-5ae6-9e7a-56ace78318f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324516} {:answer/id #uuid "dda0e0ba-dab0-5877-a01a-860314071bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325905} {:answer/id #uuid "e7302783-d593-5c2c-812c-0a52e1e00077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326004} {:answer/id #uuid "ef81183b-9c91-5ee2-9a51-4f1ad44b92e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326083}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326083} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d219a79-bb65-5544-824f-9d942d15ccb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb7ad820-361f-5675-a375-d91857e5a50d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39188" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8+4\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-8+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+(8+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=4\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}{(1)(1)}=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4638cfef-2e48-5d74-ab48-bf41cd68013c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475992}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242999fb-6645-5ccb-8016-d94893b454bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348760} {:answer/id #uuid "492ab36c-e111-5bf5-aecb-2c775badfc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349110} {:answer/id #uuid "497ebdce-d955-50f6-9c24-16eb97943ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349113} {:answer/id #uuid "8a583dc2-aca0-5635-8def-a18f404d931b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349701} {:answer/id #uuid "fafa63b9-44ba-538d-855c-38a2f923e2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349113} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b83764-dadd-53c8-9a1e-0eaa7017e5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7f8f4ed-a707-5ef8-a488-4f074f1c04ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-81031" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "532bd258-dd75-52e0-854c-9fc4d84a23df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007c15b8-5581-5d5e-b9b9-20eee41ea6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348458} {:answer/id #uuid "082db701-17a8-5bfd-8d51-0f9db11a73c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348516} {:answer/id #uuid "1b63928e-c54b-546f-a018-4d155b2013d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348679} {:answer/id #uuid "83473f2c-636b-50f3-bc7d-a4ffeaa05151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349648} {:answer/id #uuid "9be4dc92-527b-522b-8645-b3d9e5f62909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349834} :answer-field/id #uuid "10ba579e-2517-548c-a3d7-b08e597033da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8452eaa3-7ef9-5d96-b24f-03672a6ae137" :question/math-academy-id "q-81195" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+4\\text{i})(-2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "752a7cb9-f738-503d-976c-4799c9cb6901" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a00c60-8fe9-5976-9772-04b53a4f0cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269556} {:answer/id #uuid "28840bf8-728e-529f-b8ae-9e144824501a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269633} {:answer/id #uuid "4501ee0a-1e3d-5b90-8c4a-0918a3d52df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269901} {:answer/id #uuid "6b565f0d-2ee2-522d-abd4-444d8a903c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270223} {:answer/id #uuid "99f68ace-9f93-55a7-be71-674b44605a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269901} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b48799-b04a-51df-82be-a32acaaa2f29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "015b39ab-328e-51c1-8903-e3d3a737ea7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140385" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "94fc7cb2-a156-54c9-af1e-6b820bdce7bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011ee86d-97e1-53d3-a3b6-63ede2abaf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} {:answer/id #uuid "06c071d9-5963-5866-8063-d0604b2c6652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269329} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b6a67-1956-5bd6-bf81-18c5c5717aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270464} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8004b4-6cab-5417-9d66-a6e51002060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270640} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a5fd11-7f6b-58c9-94e4-6d1dc39b30af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} :answer-field/id #uuid "21591163-e34d-5795-bc94-436e957ba0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8845c35f-ffc0-56f1-b7af-e5bdef685e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140388" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f2a7d45a-7b9b-5db0-81a3-fa5d6f0775a5"}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475441}]}
